# Day 5 — Linux Fundamentals for SOC Analyst L1

## Objectives
- Understand the Linux filesystem and terminal.
- Practise navigating directories and managing files.
- Learn commands for reading and searching logs.
- Understand users, permissions and processes.
- Investigate simulated SSH authentication events.

## Environment
- Windows laptop
- Ubuntu on WSL
- Bash terminal
- Synthetic authentication logs

## Commands Practised
`pwd`, `ls`, `cd`, `mkdir`, `cat`, `less`, `head`, `tail`, `grep`, `sort`, `uniq`, `awk`, `wc`, `chmod`, `whoami`, `id`, `ps`, `top`, `df`, `ip`

## Investigation Summary
Six failed authentication records were found in the sample log. Five targeted Alice from the same source IP. A successful public-key login for Alice was also recorded from a different source IP.

## Conclusion
The repeated failures warrant further investigation, but the synthetic data does not establish that an account was compromised.

## Key Learning
Log analysis requires filtering, counting, correlating evidence and documenting uncertainty. A suspicious pattern is not automatically proof of an attack.

## Limitations
This was a simulated lab. No production system or live attacker was investigated.